  {
    "title": "Fighting Government Overreach",
    "url": "https://www.repjoed.com/News/38811/Press-Releases/Fighting-Government-Overreach",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Fighting Government Overreach\nSeptember 24, 2026\nI recently joined more than 80 of my fellow House members in submitting formal comments to the Independent Regulatory Review Commission (IRRC) calling for the withdrawal of the Pennsylvania Department of Health’s proposed rewrite of regulations that govern communicable and noncommunicable diseases in the Commonwealth.\nThe 502-page proposal\nis reminiscent of COVID lockdown-era policy and represents an unprecedented expansion of bureaucratic control, threatening the constitutional liberties, medical privacy and parental rights of citizens across the Commonwealth. Our formal comment letter highlights several alarming provisions within the proposal, including:\n•\nBroadly expanding mandatory vaccine reporting and allowing increased access to private medical records.\n•\nGranting health officials the authority to enter school buildings and interview minor students privately for disease-control investigations without clear parental knowledge or consent.\n•\nProviding the ability to enter private residences for disease control investigations while remaining silent about whether a search warrant is required prior to entering a residence.\n•\nExpanding protocols for contact tracing, isolation and quarantine, while raising concerns over unilateral executive powers to re-impose mask mandates or other coercive measures.\n•\nAttempting to permanently establish sweeping public health powers through the regulatory process rather than through open legislative debate.\nClick here to read our comment letter.\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "This legislator joined over 80 other Pennsylvania House members in submitting formal written comments to the Independent Regulatory Review Commission urging withdrawal of a proposed Pennsylvania Department of Health regulation overhaul governing communicable and noncommunicable diseases. The comments object to specific provisions in the 502-page proposal related to vaccine reporting, access to medical records, authority to enter schools and homes, and expanded quarantine and contact tracing powers. No legislation was introduced; the action was a formal public comment in an administrative review process.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "healthcare",
        "public_safety",
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "D’Orsie Announces $1.33M in State Grants for Infrastructure, Workforce, Public Safety Projects",
    "url": "https://www.repjoed.com/News/38729/Press-Releases/D-Orsie-Announces--1-33M-in-State-Grants-for-Infrastructure--Workforce--Public-Safety-Projects",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "D’Orsie Announces $1.33M in State Grants for Infrastructure, Workforce, Public Safety Projects\nSeptember 15, 2026\nHARRISBURG - Rep. Joe D’Orsie (R-Manchester) today announced over $1.3 million in Local Share Account (LSA) grants have been approved by the Commonwealth Financing Authority (CFA) to support infrastructure improvements, public safety projects and work-force training across the 47th District.\nLocal Share Account grants are funded through gaming revenues under Act 71 of 2004 and are used to support economic development, community improvement and public interest projects.\n“This funding will help our district address important needs and improve local infrastructure. I’m glad to see this gaming revenue come back to work for the communities that need them,” said D’Orsie.\nThe approved projects include:\n•\nConewago Township\n- $147,250 to purchase a Caterpillar X420 XE backhoe for Conewago Township’s public works department.\n•\nHellam Township\n- $32,000 to purchase and install six in car dash cameras for Hellam Township’s police vehicles. These cameras will be used across the fleet that provides policing services to Hellam Township, Hellam Borough and Wrightsville Borough.\n•\nManchester Township on behalf of York Area United Fire and Rescue (YAUFR\n)- $450,000 to replace all portable radio equipment used by York Area United Fire and Rescue (YAUFR) in Manchester, Spring Garden and Springettsbury townships.\n•\nSpringettsbury Township\n- $129,700 to purchase a new skid steer with a mill head attachment for Springettsbury Township.\n•\nWrightsville Borough\n- $171,948 to renovate the historic American Legion Post 469 in Wrightsville Borough. This will be the first major renovation in 20 years and includes upgrades to the post’s electrical, HVAC and roofing systems.\n•\nWrightsville Borough Municipal Authority\n- $174,915 to replace 450 linear feet of outdated and undersized 2 inch and 4 inch waterlines with a new 6 inch line along Water Street from Willow to Front streets in Wrightsville Borough. The upgrade will provide reliable water service to the borough’s sanitary sewer treatment plant, which serves approximately 4,000 residents in Wrightsville Borough and parts of Hellam Township.\n•\nYork County Economic Alliance\n, on behalf of the Children’s Home of York (CHY)- $131,063 to purchase three new all wheel drive vehicles for the Children’s Home of York (CHY) in Hellam Township. The upgraded vehicles will replace outdated units and improve the safety and reliability of transportation for youth in CHY’s care. CHY vehicles travel more than 500,000 miles each year to transport children to medical appointments, school, family visits and other essential activities.\n•\nYork County Economic Alliance\n,\non behalf of the Manufacturers Association of South-Central Pennsylvania\n- $90,350 to purchase new pneumatic and hydraulics training equipment for the Manufacturers’ Association of South-Central Pennsylvania at its Center of Excellence for Automation Training in Manchester Township. The new equipment will support hands on instruction for registered apprentices and incumbent workers seeking to upskill, allowing trainees to practice the same technologies they will encounter in the field.\nThe Commonwealth Financing Authority is an independent agency responsible for administering state investments that support Pennsylvania’s economic growth and community development.\nRepresentative Joe D’Orsie\n47th District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Novalea Eckenroad\n717.772.3161\nneckenroad@pahousegop.com\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "The Commonwealth Financing Authority approved $1.33 million in state gaming revenue grants for eight projects in Pennsylvania's 47th District. The grants will fund equipment purchases, infrastructure upgrades, and workforce training for local governments, fire and rescue services, and nonprofit organizations. Rep. D'Orsie announced the approvals but did not personally direct or vote on the funding, which was decided by an independent state agency.",
      "action_type": "other",
      "topics": [
        "infrastructure",
        "public_safety",
        "economy"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Topper Calls on Shapiro Administration to Withdraw Oppressive Lockdown-Era Regulations",
    "url": "https://www.repjoed.com/News/38632/Press-Releases/Topper-Calls-on-Shapiro-Administration-to-Withdraw-Oppressive-Lockdown-Era-Regulations",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Topper Calls on Shapiro Administration to Withdraw Oppressive Lockdown-Era Regulations\nSeptember 3, 2026\nHARRISBURG – Pennsylvania House Republican Leader Jesse Topper (R-Bedford/Fulton) today called on the Shapiro administration to withdraw proposed regulations reminiscent of COVID lockdown-era government policy that would infringe upon constitutional norms and civil liberties.\n“Oppressive COVID-era policies were viewed with skepticism by Pennsylvanians of every demographic. Submitting these regulations seems to be devoid of any understanding of our recent history of dangerous government overreach and the resulting public backlash,” Topper said.\n“Government policy that can result in serious interruptions to student success, individual privacy, and how our economy operates should be handled through the legislative process instead of through regulation. We learned that during COVID and we should be practicing that now. As such, I call on the Shapiro administration to immediately withdraw these proposed regulations and work collaboratively with the legislature on their vision for public health management.”\nAs reported this week by\nThe Daily Wire,\nthe Department of Health’s recently submitted proposed regulations would allow government officials to institute school or community mask mandates, enter private residences without warrants, and seek private health information without consent.\nFurthermore, the Department of Health would have the authority to enter any school building to interview a student of any age in private, without notification to the parent or guardian, to interview the student for contact tracing and partner services.\nTopper noted this private interview could lead to a student being convinced to take a vaccine or other treatment without notification to the parent or guardian.\n“One of the principal lessons learned through the unilateral approach taken to mitigating the COVID-19 pandemic was that the people demand their civil rights and individual freedom over government mandates,” Topper said.\n“While there may be room for improvement in managing matters of public health and the spread of communicable diseases, we can never trade constitutional protections and personal liberty for the heavy hand of government.”\nRepresentative Joe D’Orsie\n47th District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Melissa Fox\n717.260.6522\nmefox@pahousegop.com\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Pennsylvania House Republican Leader Jesse Topper issued a statement calling on the Shapiro administration to withdraw proposed Department of Health regulations. The regulations, as described in the release, would allow officials to mandate masks in schools and communities, enter private residences without warrants, collect private health information without consent, and interview students without parental notification for contact tracing purposes. Topper urged that such policy changes go through the legislative process rather than be implemented through administrative regulation.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "public_safety",
        "healthcare",
        "education"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "D’Orsie to Introduce Legislation Targeting the Grooming of Minors",
    "url": "https://www.repjoed.com/News/38514/Press-Releases/D-Orsie-to-Introduce-Legislation-Targeting-the-Grooming-of-Minors",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "D’Orsie to Introduce Legislation Targeting the Grooming of Minors\nAugust 17, 2026\nHARRISBURG - Rep. Joe D’Orsie (R-Manchester) today announced plans to introduce legislation establishing a dedicated offense within Pennsylvania’s Crimes Code to hold adults accountable when they exploit positions of authority and trust to groom minors in their care.\nWhile Pennsylvania law provides criminal penalties for sexual offenses and unlawful contact with minors, D’Orsie notes the Crimes Code has a significant gap when it comes to intervening before grooming escalates into physical abuse or explicit sexual solicitation.\n“Predators rarely cross the line all at once. They break down boundaries, build secrecy and manipulate a child’s trust long before overt sexual crimes occur. We need to give law enforcement the tools to intervene before a child is harmed, not wait until the damage is done,” D’Orsie said.\nThe proposed legislation would establish the offense of Grooming of a Minor, making it a crime for an adult responsible for a minor’s welfare to intentionally engage in a course of conduct designed to entice or facilitate prohibited sexual conduct. The bill would specifically address conduct that cultivates secrecy, inappropriate intimacy or dependency between an adult and a minor.\nTo ensure the legislation does not criminalize legitimate relationships between children and trusted adults, the proposal includes safe harbors for ordinary, transparent interactions that are incidental to educational, athletic, religious and professional roles.\n“The need to protect our children is not political. As a dad, I know there is nothing more important than making sure our children are safe from those who would take advantage. As a legislator, I have a responsibility to make sure our laws give law enforcement the tools to intervene before grooming escalates into abuse. We must close this gap now and protect our children,” D’Orsie said.\nRepresentative Joe D’Orsie\n47th District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Novalea Eckenroad\n717.772.3161\nneckenroad@pahousegop.com\nRepJoeD.com\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Rep. Joe D'Orsie announced plans to introduce a bill that would create a new criminal offense in Pennsylvania for adults who groom minors in their care. The legislation would target grooming behavior — such as cultivating secrecy or inappropriate dependency — before it escalates to physical abuse, while including protections for normal adult-child relationships in educational, athletic, religious, and professional settings. No bill has been introduced yet; this is an announcement of intent.",
      "action_type": "bill_introduced",
      "topics": [
        "public_safety"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Voting AGAINST the Early Release of Convicted Murderers.",
    "url": "https://www.repjoed.com/News/38412/Press-Releases/Voting-AGAINST-the-Early-Release-of-Convicted-Murderers",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Voting AGAINST the Early Release of Convicted Murderers.\nAugust 3, 2026\nLast week, the House majority passed a bill that would allow convicted felony murderers serving life without parole to become eligible for release. I VOTED NO.\nIn March, the Pennsylvania Supreme Court struck down the mandatory life-without-parole sentence for second-degree murder. The court gave the General Assembly 120 days to create a new sentencing framework. The House voted last week, just days before the deadline, to remove the mandatory minimums entirely, lowering the minimum sentence to zero months.\nA workable, balanced bill was possible. However, just days before the deadline, House Democrats added reckless provisions that removed mandatory minimums and created a broad medical parole process, potentially allowing dangerous offenders to seek release for illnesses such as COVID-19. These changes pose risks to public safety and retraumatize victims’ families.\nWe need a responsible solution that protects law-abiding Pennsylvanians and holds criminals accountable.\nHouse Bill 1042\nis not that solution.\nHere is the VOTE.\nRead statements of opposition below from:\nPennsylvania Law Enforcement-\nPennsylvania District Attorneys Association.\nPennsylvania Attorney General Dave Sunday.\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "A Pennsylvania state representative voted against House Bill 1042, which was passed by the House majority in response to a Pennsylvania Supreme Court ruling that struck down mandatory life-without-parole sentences for second-degree murder. The bill removes mandatory minimum sentences for felony murder convictions and expands medical parole eligibility. The legislator opposed the bill, arguing it poses public safety risks.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "public_safety",
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
